    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "description",
      "value": "Design a systematic numerical experiment to test the hypothesis that $N_c(w)$ undergoes a phase transition at $\\rho_c = w/H$. 1. Define a target function with known algebraic rank (e.g., polynomial interpolation or a trigonometric series) to establish a ground-truth \"rigid\" regime where $N_c(w)$ is small and finite. 2. Implement a sweep over constraint counts $w$ and hidden layer widths $N$. For each $(w, N)$, train a small feedforward network to satisfy constraints with max absolute error $\\le 10^{-3}$ on held-out points. 3. Determine $N_c(w)$ as the minimum $N$ achieving zero violation for each $w$. 4. Plot $N_c(w)$ vs $w$ and check for the predicted scaling behavior (sub-linear/rigid vs divergent/fat) relative to the capacity limit $H$. 5. Compare results against the algebraic rank lower bound to validate the rigidity claim."
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "skill",
      "value": "(none)"
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "expected_prediction",
      "value": "The experiment will reveal that for small $w$, $N_c(w)$ is small and finite (rigid regime), but as $w$ approaches the network's degrees of freedom $H$, $N_c(w)$ will diverge or fail to converge within practical widths, confirming the phase transition at $\\rho_c \\approx 1$."
    },
